{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact 09ff3809c56b46845e1ba28bacab5f71b891b159, all 28 changed paths, results-note artifact 82fbbceb first. Reviewed in a separate detached checkout: npm ci, npm run check (0), npm test (150/150), npm run test:protocol (74/74, 73 fixtures identical in Node and Chromium), later-stage commands exit 1. Independent adversarial pass with a hand-rolled DAG-CBOR encoder and node:crypto, about 80 adversarial probes, and 31 guard-removal mutants; I reproduced the two survivors below on the checkout myself.\n\nBlocking, both coverage gaps rather than code defects:\n1. The entry-to-anchor binding has no red proof. Deleting the verifyEntry check that the entry's app, genesis and sequencerKey equal the pinned anchor (log.ts, the 'Entry differs from the pinned sequencer/application' refusal) leaves all 73 fixtures green. Today a self-signed entry naming the actor's own key as sequencerKey is refused by that line, so the code is right, but the S1 gate's \"altered predecessors, duplicate or skipped positions\" family does not pin the sequencer authority boundary. The validateHead target check survives the same way. Add fixtures: an entry sequenced under a foreign sequencer key, an entry for another app or genesis, and a head targeting another anchor, each expected to fail with 'target'.\n2. The P-256 and canonical did:key guard on the verify path is load-bearing and untested. Removing the key() call in proof() leaves the corpus green, and @atcute/crypto's verifySigWithDidKey accepts a secp256k1 did:key with a secp256k1 signature over the same intent bytes, so without that guard a secp256k1 actor verifies, contradicting docs/protocol.md. The existing 'wrong curve rejected' fixture exercises only signIntent's signer check. Add verify-path fixtures: a secp256k1-signed intent and a non-canonical (uncompressed or wrong-multicodec) did:key, each expected to fail with 'key'.\n\nNon-blocking: the Lexicon changed-content check in schemas.ts, the codec and canonical-string checks in wire.ts link(), the sequence() sequencer check, the action regex, the payload-object check and the canonical-base64 check have no pinning fixture (each is covered indirectly by the library or by a later check); Anchor.from does not compare genesis.profile to the runtime descriptor CID, which the docs defer to S3, so a host will anchor an app pinned to a foreign profile until then; protocol.md and the wire_cid message say SHA-256 is required but the digest restriction is enforced inside @atcute/cid rather than by link().\n\nVerified: the hand-rolled encoder reproduces all seven vector blocks, digests and CIDs byte for byte; decode refuses duplicate and unordered keys, indefinite lengths, trailing bytes, floats, undefined, CIDv0 and non-canonical links, and verification checks the original bytes rather than re-encoded content at both the CBOR and Lexicon layers; signatures are P-256 with inclusive low-S, single hash, $type and version inside the signed map, and app and genesis bound to the anchor so replay against another app or genesis fails; chain verification refuses truncation, forks, duplicate and skipped positions, wrong predecessors and head mismatch independent of arrival order; retry identity is (app, actorKey, nonce) bound to the unsigned-intent CID, with two valid signatures resolving to one receipt and different content under one nonce refused; the vector writer imports only node:crypto, @ipld/dag-cbor and multiformats and no atseq or atcute code; experiments/protocol.json source hashes and the descriptor's five runtime source hashes match HEAD; no secrets, network access or non-registry dependencies.\n\nArchitecture: protocol layer (architecture sections 5 and 6) added under the plan's S1; runtime, definition and interaction layers unchanged, with the runtime descriptor pinning S0 sources. Contracts preserved. Security: the two blocking items are the trust boundary between sequencer and actor and between admitted and foreign key types; the code holds both today, and the request is for the proofs that keep them held. Everything else on the boundary is verified above. Simplification: nothing to cut; the wrapper around Lexicon closedness is the minimum the closed shapes need.\n\nOn the recut: add the fixtures, republish all path artifacts at the new head with the results note first, and request fresh review. Nothing here adopts the plan-review recommendations.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:82fbbceb0c5587acd85148ab79ee19411bb38927","head":"09ff3809c56b46845e1ba28bacab5f71b891b159","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:8deb5e586973ec0e2a4efd4b3b0d1c56ca9176e5\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:8deb5e586973ec0e2a4efd4b3b0d1c56ca9176e5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:06472e393830732a89b844acc2c4fd6937c54bad, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:0bc68c0d7a8310474bfa2d8cfcbe8ca8f155f0fc, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:14621d953242bca28f589a4b475653b06eb09726, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:23556b96611ca1334ddc33235c104a8b2231a4a9, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:2505a3bff6e62548bd3d5662704d9952be4fdc50, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:290fe5eb476d0971329b7145b5f8b78ccb4b6ff3, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:2a907cacfd1a21a8c2204df85f8e2ead86b2c189, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:3026bef3b1a7ac1e9622e3a824aa1b7f841e352e, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:356a43690ac886825c4aaaf24f898cc83437c198, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:43a3d14d99498e60e325aa0af2146a639771b8a7, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sh